DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The debate over Herder's relationship to the Enlightenment has been structured
largely by Isaiah Berlin's influential framing of the "Counter-Enlightenment."
Berlin depicted Herder, alongside Vico and Hamann, as founding critics of
Enlightenment universalism, abstract rationalism, and the idea of unilinear
progress. Berlin's thesis generated a major historiographical controversy: Robert
Norton challenged the very existence of a coherent "Counter-Enlightenment"
movement as a scholarly myth, while Eva Piirimae has argued that Berlin
misreads Herder by aligning him too closely with reactionary anti-Enlightenment
positions. Revisionist scholars (Sikka, James, Denby) tend to position Herder
as an internal critic or reformer of the Enlightenment rather than its opponent,
someone who shares Enlightenment commitments to human flourishing and empirical
inquiry while rejecting its abstract universalism and teleological progress
narratives.

The Kant-Herder controversy is a second major axis of this literature. Kant's
hostile reviews of the first two parts of Herder's Ideen (1784-85) in the
Allgemeine Literatur-Zeitung inaugurated a profound intellectual rupture between
a former student and his teacher. The dispute touched on methodology (empirical
vs. transcendental), teleology (Herder's organismic natural teleology vs. Kant's
regulative use of teleological judgment), the concept of race, and the status
of reason in history. Zammito's magisterial study traces the longer arc of this
disagreement through the formation of Kant's Critique of Judgment and the birth
of both anthropology and biology as disciplinary formations. Zuckert's close
reading of Kant's first Herder review illuminates the specific metaphysical
stakes. Nassar examines how the analogy between organic wholes and history
structures both thinkers' philosophies of nature. Gjesdal's work connects
Herder's hermeneutical approach -- attending to the particular, the historical,
and the embodied -- with his critique of Enlightenment rationalism.

Recent scholarship has focused on reconstructing Herder's pre-critical
intellectual formation under Kant in Konigsberg (1762-64) and on his
ambivalent relationship to the Aufklarung. Forster's comprehensive philosophical
monograph argues that Herder is neither a Counter-Enlightenment figure nor
simply an Enlightenment radical but a distinct philosophical voice who transforms
inherited concepts. Beiser situates Herder within the broader trajectory of
German philosophy from Kant to historicism, emphasizing how Herder's challenge
to Kantian critique forced responses that shaped the development of German
Idealism.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain directly addresses the central intellectual-historical questions of
Herder's philosophy of history: what he rejects in Enlightenment historiography,
how his disagreements with Kant define his position, and whether his cultural
pluralism constitutes a coherent alternative to Enlightenment universalism. The
Kant-Herder dispute over teleology and reason in history is foundational for
understanding the Ideen as a philosophical text.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Scholarship on Herder's specific engagement with Voltaire's philosophy of history
(as opposed to broader Aufklarung critique) remains thin in English-language
secondary literature. The question of Herder's early (pre-1774) relationship to
Kant's precritical natural philosophy has received less sustained attention than
the post-Critique disputes.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The Berlin/revisionist debate should be presented as a live interpretive
controversy, with Norton and Piirimae as key revisionist voices against Berlin's
framing, and Sikka and James as nuanced middle positions. The Kant-Herder
controversy deserves its own subsection focused on the Ideen reviews and the
teleology dispute.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Counter-Enlightenment reading (Berlin): 2 papers -- Herder as foundational
  anti-Enlightenment thinker rejecting universalism, reason, and progress
- Revisionist/Enlightenment radical reading: 5 papers -- Herder as internal
  critic or reformer of Enlightenment, sharing its empirical and humanistic
  commitments
- Kant-Herder controversy (teleology, race, anthropology): 4 papers --
  examining the dispute over the Ideen reviews and their philosophical stakes
- Comprehensive monographs: 5 books -- covering the full philosophical context
====================================================================
}

@book{zammito2002kant,
  author = {Zammito, John H.},
  title = {Kant, Herder, and the Birth of Anthropology},
  year = {2002},
  publisher = {University of Chicago Press},
  address = {Chicago},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Zammito argues that the Kant-Herder controversy of the 1780s,
  catalyzed by Kant's hostile reviews of the Ideen, generated the conceptual
  space in which both anthropology and biology emerged as modern disciplines.
  The dispute was not merely personal but reflected deep divergences over the
  nature of empirical inquiry, teleology in nature and history, the concept of
  race, and the relationship between natural history and philosophical
  speculation. Herder's organismic and genetic approach to human history
  challenged Kant's transcendental framework at every level.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most sustained scholarly examination of the
  Kant-Herder relationship, tracing the intellectual formation of both thinkers
  from Herder's student years in Konigsberg through the 1790s. It is
  indispensable for understanding what is philosophically at stake in Kant's
  reviews of the Ideen and how Herder's philosophy of history diverges from
  Kantian commitments on teleology, race, and reason. The book directly
  addresses all four key questions of this domain.

  POSITION: Locates the Kant-Herder dispute as generative of key modern
  disciplines; neither side wholly vindicated.
  },
  keywords = {Kant-Herder controversy, teleology, anthropology, Ideen reviews, High},

}

